---
import { getEntry, render } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const parts = [
  {
    id: "code",
    title: "The Code",
    meta: "Canonical. The portable normative core.",
    file: "CODE.md",
  },
  {
    id: "charter",
    title: "The Charter",
    meta: "Operational elaboration. The Code governs on conflict.",
    file: "CHARTER.md",
  },
  {
    id: "commentary",
    title: "The Commentary",
    meta: "Non-normative. It explains; it does not govern.",
    file: "COMMENTARY.md",
  },
] as const;

const rendered = await Promise.all(
  parts.map(async (part) => {
    const entry = await getEntry("docs", part.id);
    if (!entry) throw new Error(`Missing document: ${part.id}`);
    const { Content } = await render(entry);
    return { ...part, Content };
  }),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Harder to Fool"
  description="A code of conduct for human-machine truth-seeking: a joint process harder to fool than either participant alone. The complete text of the Code, the Charter, and the Commentary."
  path="/"
>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="page">
    <header class="masthead">
      <hr class="masthead__rule" />
      <h1 class="masthead__monument">Harder to&nbsp;Fool</h1>
      <p class="masthead__sub">
        A code of conduct for human&#8209;machine truth&#8209;seeking.
      </p>
      <p class="masthead__tenets">
        Reality is the reference.<br />
        Models are instruments.<br />
        Confidence must be earned.<br />
        Correction is progress.
      </p>
      <nav class="masthead__contents" aria-label="Contents">
        {parts.map((part) => <a href={`#${part.id}`}>{part.title}</a>)}
        <a href="#colophon">Use</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <div class="preface col">
      <p>
        Humans and machine systems now reach conclusions together, and the
        failure mode is quiet. A model writes just as fluently when it is
        wrong, and its agreement is easy to mistake for evidence. When the
        result is bad, nobody is quite sure who decided.
      </p>
      <p>
        Harder to Fool is a short discipline for that work. Six rules for
        the process, ten for the machine, and one test for any claim that
        matters: <em>name the observation that would change your mind.</em>
        Every rule has an ID, so saying &ldquo;K3 applies&rdquo; opens a
        correction rather than winning an argument.
      </p>
      <p>
        Models are already trained to hedge and admit uncertainty, so a code
        like this can look redundant. Surgeons said the same about
        checklists: they already knew every item. Checklists cut error rates
        anyway, because they interrupt momentum and give the nurse standing
        to challenge the surgeon. The rule IDs do that job here. A trained
        tendency is not reliable behavior, and under pressure a model still
        agrees too easily. Two words give anyone at the table, human or
        machine, standing to object before a mistake ships.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure to watch for is theater: a model can complete
        &ldquo;we would revise this if we observed&nbsp;___&rdquo; with
        something plausible every time. The blank only counts when someone
        reads the answer and asks whether anyone expects to observe it. The
        framework predicts this failure of itself, in writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Adopting it is plumbing, not ceremony. Copy the documents into your
        repository, point your agents file at them, and add the small script
        that fails CI when the copy drifts from the canonical text. There is
        nothing to join and no rank to hold. And if honest use never changes
        a decision, the Code says to archive it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    rendered.map((part) => (
      <article class="part" id={part.id}>
        <div class="running-head">
          <div class="page running-head__inner">
            <span>{part.title}</span>
            <a class="mono-link" href={`/${part.file}`}>
              {part.file}
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="page">
          <header class="part__head col">
            <h2 class="part__title">{part.title}</h2>
            <p class="part__meta">{part.meta}</p>
          </header>
          <div class="prose col">
            <part.Content />
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))
  }

  <section class="colophon" id="colophon">
    <div class="running-head">
      <div class="page running-head__inner">
        <span>Use</span>
        <a class="mono-link" href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="page">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 class="colophon__title">Use</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>In a repository,</strong> vendor the documents under
          <code>.ai/harder-to-fool/</code> and merge the AGENTS.md block: it
          inlines the Kernel verbatim, points back at the canonical files,
          and check_sync.py fails the build if the block drifts from the
          Code. Cite rules by ID: &ldquo;K3 applies&rdquo; opens a
          correction, it does not end an argument. <strong>In a consequential
          collaboration,</strong> run the Charter&rsquo;s operating cycle and
          keep its compact decision record. <strong>In a low-stakes
          task,</strong> apply the principles proportionately; do not turn a
          simple question into a ceremony.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>By a machine system:</strong> every document is served as
          plain markdown, indexed in llms.txt. A system without persistent
          identity or memory applies the Code within the current context only.
          Implementation is not belief, consent, or enduring commitment.
        </p>
        <p>
          The documents are licensed CC BY 4.0: use them anywhere, credit
          Harder to Fool with a link, and mark your changes, which is the
          Revision rule as license terms. The tooling is MIT.
        </p>
        <ul class="colophon__files">
          <li>
            <a href="/CODE.md">CODE.md</a>
            <span>the canonical text</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/CHARTER.md">CHARTER.md</a>
            <span>the operating procedure</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/COMMENTARY.md">COMMENTARY.md</a>
            <span>the rationale</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/AGENTS.md">AGENTS.md</a>
            <span>a repository template, Kernel inlined verbatim</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/check_sync.py">check_sync.py</a>
            <span>fails the build when the block drifts from the Code</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
            <span>the machine index</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a>
            <span>the complete corpus in one fetch</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/LICENSE.txt">LICENSE.txt</a>
            <span>CC BY 4.0 for the documents, MIT for the tooling</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="colophon__end">
          <svg class="colophon__device" viewBox="0 0 32 32" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="6" y="7" width="3.5" height="18"></rect>
            <rect x="14.25" y="7" width="3.5" height="18"></rect>
            <rect x="22.5" y="7" width="3.5" height="18"></rect>
          </svg>
          <p class="colophon__close">
            The repository history is the revision record.<br />
            Forks disclose their source and substantive modifications.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
